                                                                                "title":  "Interest Rates and Stock Prices: An Old Debate Flares up!",
                                                                                "source":  "Aswath Damodaran",
                                                                                "author":  "Aswath Damodaran",
                                                                                "url":  "https://aswathdamodaran.blogspot.com/2026/09/interest-rates-and-stock-prices-old.html",
                                                                                "date":  "2026-09-10",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgDslAhzkZu_Oq6rigZLlCaVn-tfCOa1vu3ybROAgHuWdrNTjeWjdAqX6_6ZHMIDNlYg6MmxdA-NciCoeOAwGWdq2GoeHsO1qvCXbrDMAMDkl9uJ5b2cR0cMQAqoH-hEAjvSIA1y6Z3kqp31L-8KS0qvM7hdLfxbLCtu17Ke766hvMmqsGR26fCLqJtnaA/s1718/USTreasuryRates2026.jpg",
                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran revisits the long-running debate over whether rising interest rates necessarily hurt stock prices, finding the relationship is far more nuanced than the standard textbook narrative. With US Treasury rates at 20-year highs and corporate borrowing costs elevated, he notes that US equities have remained resilient due to surging corporate earnings growth. He argues the impact of rate changes on company value depends heavily on whether the rate move is driven by inflation expectations or real growth, and on how each company\u0027s cash flows respond. The analysis spans domestic and international bond markets, default spreads, and sector-level equity data to show why blanket rules about rates and stocks consistently break down.",
                                                                                "take":  "Damodaran\u0027s core point ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â that the directionality of rates matters less than the reason behind them ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â is the right frame for investors who reflexively sell equities every time the 10-year moves up. In a world where rates are rising because nominal growth is strong, equities can and do outperform. The danger in 2026 is that markets have priced in the \u0027growth-driven rate\u0027 story without adequately stress-testing the \u0027inflation persistence\u0027 alternative. Investors should use Damodaran\u0027s framework to audit their own holdings: high-duration growth names remain vulnerable even if the index holds up, because earnings momentum is not uniformly distributed.",
                                                                                "isNew":  true
